    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Stone edging is the structural backbone of a well-designed garden. Whether it is rugged fieldstone, crisp granite or classic limestone, a stone border draws a definite line between beds and lawn, stops soil and mulch drifting onto the grass and gives the whole garden a more finished look, adding to the kerb appeal of the front garden too. But stone is not a "set it and forget it" feature. The weather, from winter's freeze-thaw cycles to vigorous summer root growth, is constantly working to shift, stain and wear your edging.</p>
<p>A simple seasonal routine keeps that in check. By following a structured approach through the year, you can head off long-term damage, keep the edge safe to walk and mow beside, and keep it looking as good as the day it was laid.</p>

<!--figure:garden-stone-path|A curved stone garden path through flowering beds-->

<h2 id="spring-renewal">1. Spring renewal</h2>
<p>Spring is the most important season for stone edging. Once the ground thaws, the main job is dealing with frost heave: water in the soil freezes and expands, physically pushing stones up or out of their original line.</p>
<p>As the ground softens, start with a thorough inspection. Walk the full length of the edging and look for stones that have tilted, lifted or sunk. Use a rubber mallet to tap slightly heaved stones gently back into line. If a section has moved significantly, lift the stones, re-level the sand or gravel bed underneath and reset them.</p>
<p>Spring is also the time for a proper clean. Winter tends to leave a layer of silt, salt residue and green slime. Scrub the stones with a stiff brush and a bucket of water with a little mild detergent. If you use a pressure washer, keep it on a low setting with a fan nozzle and follow the stone supplier's guidance; avoid it altogether on soft stone such as sandstone or limestone, which it can etch, opening up the surface so that moss and algae take hold more easily later.</p>
<p>Finally, check the joints. If your edging has polymeric sand or mortar between the stones, look for cracks caused by the winter cold and repair them early, before weed seeds settle in.</p>

<h2 id="summer-vigilance">2. Summer vigilance</h2>
<p>Through the summer the focus shifts from structure to keeping things neat. With warm weather and regular showers, weeds and creeping grasses will try to cross your stone borders.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use a half-moon edger, long-handled edging shears or a strimmer to stop the lawn creeping over the stones.</li>
<li>Pull weeds growing in the joints as soon as you see them, getting the root out.</li>
<li>If you use vinegar-based products, including "horticultural vinegar", treat them as a weedkiller: follow the label, keep them off the plants you want to keep and out of drains.</li>
</ul>
<p>Summer is also when ants are most active. Some species like the cool, sheltered space under edging stones. Ants rarely cause structural problems on their own, but they can undermine sand joints by carrying the sand away, which you will notice as small heaps of fine sand or soil along the edge. Brush the heaps away, refill the joints and firm them. If you choose to use an ant treatment, follow the label.</p>

<h2 id="autumn-preparation">3. Autumn preparation</h2>
<p>Autumn is mainly about clearing debris and getting the edging ready for winter. As trees drop their leaves, it is tempting to let them pile up against the stones, but wet leaves contain tannins that can stain paler stone, such as some sandstones, limestone and marble.</p>
<p>Rake or blow leaves off the borders regularly. A thick layer of wet leaves left against stone through a damp autumn holds moisture and encourages moss, algae and mould. That moisture also soaks into porous stone, which becomes a problem once the first hard frost arrives.</p>
<p>Before the ground freezes, do one last stability check. If the edging runs beside a driveway or a busy path, make sure every stone is firmly seated: loose stones are easily caught by a snow shovel in winter, which can chip the stone or damage the tool. If your stone supplier recommends sealing, a dry, mild spell in autumn is a good time to apply it, as the product label advises, so the stone has some protection against winter wet.</p>

<h2 id="winter-protection">4. Winter protection</h2>
<p>Winter is the harshest season for stone edging, yet it needs the least physical work. The aim is passive protection, and the biggest threat is de-icers. Rock salt (sodium chloride) can damage natural stone and cause spalling, where the surface flakes or peels away, and its runoff can harm the lawn and border plants alongside.</p>
<ul>
<li>Avoid rock salt near natural stone. If a path or drive beside the edging needs de-icing, use a product your stone supplier approves, and use sand or grit for grip where you can.</li>
<li>Before the first snowfall, mark the line of the edging with tall, flexible snow markers so that you, or anyone clearing snow, do not strike and dislodge the stones once they are buried.</li>
<li>Clear snow by skimming rather than scraping along the edge.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep an eye on the stones during mid-winter thaws. If water pools against the edging, try to open a route for it to drain away. Standing water that freezes and thaws over and over is a common cause of cracked and shifted stone.</p>

<h2 id="structural-repairs">5. Structural repairs</h2>
<p>Even well-kept stone edging eventually needs more than a tap with a mallet. Over time, natural settling of the ground can make sections lean or sink.</p>
<p>To reset a section properly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Lift the affected stones and set them aside in order, so they go back in the same sequence.</li>
<li>Dig the trench a little deeper and remove any soft soil.</li>
<li>Add a layer of compacted crushed stone sub-base (sold in the UK as Type 1) and compact it firmly. This gives a stable, free-draining bed that resists soil movement.</li>
<li>Reset the stones on the base, checking them with a spirit level and a string line so the run is even.</li>
<li>Backfill behind the stones with soil and firm it well.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your edging is mortared, look for stepped cracks running through the joints, a sign the ground has moved and the rigid mortar has broken. Rake out the cracked mortar and repoint with a suitable mortar for your stone, or ask a stonemason to do it. As well as restoring the look, this stops water getting into the cracks and doing more damage in the next freeze.</p>

<h2 id="deep-cleaning">6. Deep cleaning techniques</h2>
<p>Every few seasons, your edging may need more than a quick scrub. For stone that has become heavily discoloured by grime, algae or lichen, use a cleaner made for the stone type, used as the label directs. A pH-neutral stone cleaner is a sensible starting point for most stone.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Organic stains</strong> from leaves, berries or acorns usually respond to a cleaner made for organic stains on your type of stone. Strong hydrogen peroxide is hazardous, so do not improvise with it.</li>
<li><strong>Rust stains</strong> from garden tools, furniture feet or iron-rich water may need an oxalic-acid-based rust remover. Wear gloves and eye protection, follow the label, and do not use it on limestone or marble without the supplier's advice.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Always try any cleaner on a hidden test patch first. Natural stone varies chemically: a product that works well on granite can etch or mark a calcium-based stone such as limestone or marble.</p></blockquote>
<p>Once the stone is clean and completely dry, you may want to seal it, but only if your stone supplier recommends sealing for that stone. A breathable, penetrating sealer is usually preferred for outdoor stone: unlike a surface sealer that leaves a shiny film, it soaks in, lets moisture escape and helps repel water and oil.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on my stone edging?</h3>
<p>Use one with caution, if at all. A high setting can damage soft stone such as sandstone or limestone and strip away its natural weathered surface. If you do use one on harder stone, keep it on a low setting with a fan nozzle, hold it well back from the surface and follow the stone supplier's guidance.</p>
<h3>How often should I reseal my stone borders?</h3>
<p>Only seal if your stone supplier recommends it, and reapply as the product label advises. A simple check is to sprinkle some water on the stone: if it stops beading and soaks straight in, the sealer is probably wearing off.</p>
<h3>Why is my stone edging turning green?</h3>
<p>The green is likely moss, algae or lichen, which thrive in shady, damp spots. Scrub it off with a stiff brush, or use a cleaner made for the stone type, following the label. Improving light and drainage, for example by cutting back overhanging shrubs, helps stop it coming back.</p>
<h3>Is it better to mortar stone edging or leave it dry-set?</h3>
<p>Dry-setting, where stones sit on a bed of sand or gravel, is often better for garden edging because the stones can move slightly as the ground expands and contracts, and individual stones are easy to reset. Mortared edging looks more finished but is more prone to cracking if the ground moves.</p>
<h3>What is the best way to stop weeds growing between the stones?</h3>
<p>Well-filled, compacted joints are the best defence. Polymeric sand, swept into the joints and lightly misted with water as the product instructions describe, hardens into a firm joint that resists weeds while allowing a little movement. Topping up joints whenever they erode, and removing weeds while they are small, does the rest.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Stone edging rewards little and often: a spring walk round and reset, regular weeding and leaf clearing, careful winter de-icing and an occasional deep clean will keep it crisp for a long time. If you are still choosing a material, our guide to <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a> compares stone with the alternatives, <a href="/blog/brick-edging-pros-and-cons">brick edging pros and cons</a> covers a close cousin, and <a href="/blog/how-to-build-landscape-edging">how to build landscape edging</a> walks through installation step by step. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your garden with a designer.</p>
